In high school, one is taught that job of physics is to find the path of an object from knowledge of the forces that act on it. These forces lead to a differential equation which is then integrated to find the path. Lagrangian mechanics turns this on its head and seeks to find the full path directly and, once it is known, infer the forces that acted on the object. But how do we find the right path?

If we could somehow assign a single number every possible path going from a starting point to an ending point in spacetime, then maybe the value of this number would tell us the right path. [insert schematic equation: S = some invariant scalar associated with a path]. Since all observers witness the body acting in accordance to same physical laws, the number must be agreed up by all observers. Spacetime geometry only gives us one such number, the path length, or proper time, of the worldline. We also know that the length of the worldline between fixed endpoints is a measure of how much the worldline "wiggles" getting from start to finish, and this in turn represents the body's acceleration. Proper time, then, seems to be the starting point for a number we can use to compare paths. 

Once we have a number associated with each path, how do we choose the right number. The answer is familiar in machine learning. There you ask, if I knew some function that was minimized (or maximized, or on a saddle point, etc.), when the model matched the data, then I could just minimize that function. The situation here is similar. We want the action to be a function (of paths) that we can extremize to find the correct path. We don't need to know why we should expect to be extremized, but we will see that we do expect this.

The worldline proper time gives us a spacetime invariant to work with, but it alone doesn't encode the physical laws acting on a body. For that, we must also know the body's mass. We can see this easily. A body with a large mass will accelerate less when acted upon by the same force. We can ensure spacetime invariance and use our minimization procedure if we simply scale the path length by the mass: [equation: S = integral(m*dtau)]. This is, in fact, the action for a free particle, and minimizing it says that the free particle travels in straight line in spacetime, that is, at constant velocity, as expected. 

But how do we define action for particles that don't follow a straight worldline? We still need some scale factor, let's call it $L$, and name it the Lagrangian. We then have: [equation: S = integral(L(x,x-dot)ds)]. 

The attentive reader now protests, wait a second, I see where you are going. L(x,x-dot) is a scalar function whose gradient is force. It may be easier to to use this action-minimization procedure than to add to add up force at every point, but the procedure is essentially still building the path from local forces. This is true if we think of L as being nothing but a map from a force field to a scalar field. However, if we think or forces as arising from the necessity of geometry, then L encodes not local forces which have no global structure, but a space with some curvature. An analogy is that on a sphere, the shortest path between two points is a geodesic not a straight-line path between points [diagram/animation]. The function we minimize to find the geodesic is determined by the geometry of the sphere. 

