We can derive the worldline of a free particle geometrically. It is a straight line, for curvature of the worldline is proper acceleration and "free" means not accelerating. A straight line is the minimization of path length, suggesting that the correct choice of worldline is some "extremization" over the path. Combining mass and path length for a free particle, we can write the quantity to be extremized, or the action:

``` math
S_{\mathrm{free}}=-m\int d\tau.
```

For a particle that is not free, we allow for a more flexible local action-measure than mass: the Lagrangian. In proper-time form, the action can be written

``` math
S=\int L_{\tau}(x,u)\,d\tau,\qquad u=\frac{dx}{d\tau}.
```

For a scalar potential coupled to proper time, we can write

``` math
S=-\int (m+V(x))\,d\tau.
```

This offers a tidy interpretation of this version of the Lagrangian. Up to the sign, it is the local effective rest energy; the action is the local effective rest energy accumulated over proper time. In the rest frame, being at higher scalar potential adds to rest energy. While this interpretation is tidy, the Lagrangian is normally written in coordinate time. Since

``` math
d\tau=dt\sqrt{1-v^{2}},
```

the same scalar-coupled action becomes

``` math
S=-\int (m+V(x))\sqrt{1-v^{2}}\,dt.
```

In the more familiar coordinate-time potential form,

``` math
L_{t}=-m\sqrt{1-v^{2}}-V(x),
```

and in the low-speed limit,

``` math
L_{t}\approx -m+\frac12 mv^{2}-V(x).
```

Dropping the constant $`-m`$ leaves

``` math
L_{t}\approx T-V.
```

The fact that a free particle travels a straight line in spacetime also hints that, whatever the actual path is, it should be inevitable in the right geometry. In fact, we can construct spaces in which the actual path in spacetime is the projection of a geodesic in a richer geometry. The Lagrangian is not itself the projection map. Rather, the Lagrangian compactly encodes the geometry or machinery that makes that lifted path geodesic. In general notation, the Lagrangian lives on velocity phase space, technically the tangent bundle of configuration space: the space of positions and velocities.

``` math
S[\gamma]=\int_{\gamma} L(q,\dot q,t)\,dt.
```

The path is selected by requiring the action to be stationary,

``` math
\delta S=0,
```

which in coordinates gives the Euler-Lagrange equation

``` math
\frac{d}{dt}\left(\frac{\partial L}{\partial \dot q^{i}}\right)-\frac{\partial L}{\partial q^{i}}=0.
```

If someone hands us a Lagrangian, we can then hand them back a future history.

But where does the Lagrangian come from? Satisfyingly, it becomes inevitable in the structure of the more complete geometry the theory postulates, and it is mediated by covariant spacetime objects. This will be the topic of the "Gravitation" and "Gauge Theory" sections.

If we do not know the origin of the Lagrangian, what should we put in it? First, it should be a combination of terms that encode inertia and interaction: rate-dependent terms encode how hard the system is to change, while position- or field-dependent terms encode what redirects that change. Second, the terms, or their combination, should be invariant scalars, because the action itself must be invariant.

